--TEST--
`Core\Router::methodsFor`'s array holds each verb as an enum case, so a callable declaring `Core\Http\Method` accepts every element `Core\Arr` hands it and a case in a `mixed` is still a case — `rule:enums/representation`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Router::methodsFor
// The array is built outside compiled code, so each element has to carry the
// enum tag compiled code would have written. An element that is a bare `int`
// fails every reader below that checks the tag.
class Pages
{
    #[Core\Route(path: "/pages", method: Core\Http\Method::Get)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function list(): string
    {
        return "";
    }

    #[Core\Route(path: "/pages", method: Core\Http\Method::Post)]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function create(): string
    {
        return "";
    }
}

array<Core\Http\Method> $allowed = Core\Router::methodsFor("/pages");

// The `Allow` header of a `405`, in one expression. `Core\Arr::map` calls the
// callable itself, which checks each argument's tag.
echo "allow: ", Core\Str::join(Core\Arr::map($allowed, Core\Http::methodName), ", "), "\n";

// The same check through `Core\Arr::filter`.
echo "get: ", Core\Arr::count(Core\Arr::filter($allowed, fn(Core\Http\Method $m): bool => $m == Core\Http\Method::Get)) as string, "\n";

// An enum case in a `mixed` is always truthy, whatever its backing value is.
echo "truthy: ", Core\Arr::count(Core\Arr::filter($allowed, fn(mixed $m): bool => $m ? true : false)) as string, "\n";

// A case read into a `mixed` is still a case, and is not an `int`.
mixed $first = $allowed[0];
echo "is a method: ", ($first is Core\Http\Method) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
echo "is an int: ", ($first is int) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
--EXPECT--
allow: GET, POST
get: 1
truthy: 2
is a method: yes
is an int: no
